  "levels": {
    "1": {
      "title": "Claude Code saves secrets in public files",
      "text": "Claude Code is an AI tool that helps people write software. Sometimes it asks before it runs a command. A user can say \"don't ask again.\" Then the tool saves the command in a file. If the command has a password, the password is saved too. A security company found 1,122 public copies of this file with secrets inside."
    },
    "2": {
      "title": "Claude Code can save passwords in public project files",
      "text": "Researchers at the security company Apiiro say that passwords and keys are leaking from Claude Code, an AI tool for writing software. When a user chooses \"Yes, and don't ask again,\" the tool saves a rule in a project file called .claude/settings.local.json. Sometimes the rule is the exact command, and any password typed into it is saved as plain text.\n\nApiiro found 104,185 copies of this file on public GitHub. About 1.1% of them, or 1,122 copies, hold at least one secret. The researchers did not test if the secrets still work. Deleting the file does not solve the problem, because older versions stay in git history. They advise users to change every password and key first, and clean up later."
    },
    "3": {
      "title": "Claude Code approvals leave passwords and keys in public GitHub repositories",
      "text": "Security company Apiiro says that passwords and API keys typed into commands approved in Claude Code are sitting in public repositories. When a user selects \"Yes, and don't ask again,\" the assistant can save the exact command, secret included, in .claude/settings.local.json. The company found 104,185 copies of the file, and 1,122 of them, about 1.1%, contain at least one secret, for a total of 1,379 different secrets. They include 379 database passwords, 43 SSH logins, 42 tokens for GitHub, GitLab and npm, and 23 AWS keys. The researchers did not check whether the secrets still work, although one company they warned confirmed that its key was live.\n\nClaude Code adds the file to the user's global git excludes, but the researchers say this protection stays on one computer and does not help once the file has been committed. Common scanners also miss many cases: gitleaks flagged 564 of the 1,122 files, TruffleHog flagged 643, and neither flagged 215. Apiiro says four related bug reports, including one from August 2025, were closed by an automated bot, and none received a reply from Anthropic. The advice is to rotate every exposed secret first, then remove the file."
    }
  },
